<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
	PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy — BetterW4

	Apple has required this file since 1 May 2024 for any app that calls a "required reason"
	API. Uploading without it earns an ITMS-91053 "Missing API declaration" mail from App Store
	Connect and, for a new app, a rejection. Nothing in the app's behaviour depends on it; it is
	a declaration, and it has to match what the code actually does.

	Each entry below was verified against the tree rather than copied from a template. If you add
	a call to one of Apple's listed APIs, add its category here in the same pass — the check is
	static analysis over the binary, so an undeclared call is found every time.

	The two claims that are *not* required-reason API declarations, and that carry the weight of
	the App Store privacy answer, are the two at the top: this app tracks nobody and collects
	nothing. Both are literally true — BetterW4 has no backend, no analytics SDK, and no account
	of its own. See docs/RELEASE.md §2 for the full argument.
-->
<dict>
	<!-- No tracking as Apple defines it: no linking of this app's data to third-party data for
	     advertising or measurement, and no data broker. The app never calls ATTrackingManager
	     and links no ad framework. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Empty because there is no tracking. Any domain listed here would be blocked outright
	     until the user grants tracking permission; the app's one host, w4.uwcrcn.no, is the
	     college's own server and is not a tracking domain. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!-- Empty on purpose, and it is the whole privacy nutrition label. "Collect" in Apple's
	     sense means transmit off the device. Everything BetterW4 knows — the W4 session cookie,
	     cached pages, preferences — stays in the app sandbox. The only thing it ever sends is
	     the student's own credentials, back to the college's own login form. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- UserDefaults: preferences (theme, calendar style, subject colours, notification
		     toggles), the review-prompt bookkeeping in ReviewPromptStore, and the directory
		     sweep state in DirectoryRepository / DirectoryStore.

		     CA92.1 is the "app's own defaults only" reason, and it is the correct one *because*
		     the app has no app group: every one of these reads and writes lands in
		     UserDefaults.standard, which nothing outside this app can see. An app-group suite
		     would have required a different reason. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- File timestamps: AttachmentCache stamps and reads modification dates to run its LRU
		     eviction, OutgoingMessageAttachment reads them to purge stale temporary files,
		     ReviewEligibility reads the Documents directory's creation date to work out how long
		     the app has been installed, and W4PageCache / MessageCacheManager enumerate file
		     attributes to report cache size in Settings.

		     C617.1 is the "files inside the app container" reason. Every path above is under the
		     app's own Caches, Documents or temporary directory — the app reads no timestamp it
		     did not write. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Deliberately absent, each checked against the tree on 2026-08-18:

		     • DiskSpace — the caches measure themselves with NSURLFileSizeKey, which is a file
		       size and is not in Apple's disk-space category. Nothing calls statfs, and nothing
		       reads NSURLVolumeAvailableCapacityKey or NSFileSystemFreeSize. Do not add this
		       entry "to be safe": an unused declaration is its own kind of wrong answer.
		     • SystemBootTime — no systemUptime, no kern.boottime.
		     • ActiveKeyboards — no activeInputModes.
		-->
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
